  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of an all-solid-state battery, illustrated in a simplified cross-section between an anode on the left and a cathode on the right. A car at the top suggests the application is electric vehicles, and the external circuit shows electrons flowing from the negative side toward the positive side during operation.\n\nThe central message is that battery performance depends strongly on what happens inside the solid electrolyte and at its interfaces. The electrolyte is drawn as a densely packed mixture of particles, with gray and blue spheres representing different phases or materials distributed between the electrodes. Insets highlight several key features: rough, imperfect contact at the anode interface; grain boundaries within the polycrystalline electrolyte; a cathode interface where neighboring particles interact; and ion transport through the solid. Yellow arrows inside the electrolyte emphasize multidirectional ion movement through the particle network.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that charge transport in solid-state batteries is controlled not just by bulk ionic conduction, but also by microstructure, particle contacts, and interfacial resistance at both electrodes. It visually compares these bottlenecks to show that interfaces and grain boundaries are major factors limiting efficient ion flow across the cell.",
